TypeScript adaptations add bounded asynchronous ReadHandle reads, typed sample
indexes, lazy owned packet payloads, cancellation and explicit resource budgets.
Negative CTTS offset handling follows the pinned reference, including signed
32-bit interpretation for versions 0 and 1 and DTS shift estimation excluding
the last two table entries. Reference comparisons include packet timestamps and
complete payload bytes. This notice does not constitute a project license audit.

Current scope: local non-fragmented video/audio sample tables with one description
and a single full-duration unit-rate edit. Versioned audio entries, fragmented media, general edit
lists, decryption, compressed decoding, MP4 muxing and CLI integration remain
unfinished. This implementation does not establish complete MOV/MP4 compatibility.

Leading empty edit accumulation, default ELST entry-count recovery and stream
start times follow mov_read_elst/mov_build_index/mov_fix_index at that commit.

Default video edit rate handling follows mov_read_elst (unsigned16.16 read) and
mov_fix_index (stored rate unused) at the pinned FFmpeg commit. This adaptation
validates field bounds without rejecting non-unit rate words.

Default negative video media-time handling follows mov_read_elst and
get_edit_list_entry/mov_fix_index at the pinned commit: strict-only rejection is
separate from default parsing; duration overflow uses unsigned64-bit addition.
An input INT64_MIN search position is distinct from output timestamp sentinels.

AAC leading delay, partial-frame preroll classification and final discard
padding follow pinned mov.c mov_fix_index/mov_finalize_packet. AAC-LC nominal
frequency is read from the AudioSpecificConfig frequency-index table; codec
frame samples and packet timebase remain separate quantities.

AAC multiple edit index reconstruction in src/mp4/audio-edit.ts adapts
mov_fix_index/mov_finalize_packet from pinned libavformat/mov.c and skip-sample
injection from libavformat/demux.c, under LGPL-2.1-or-later at the commit above.
It preserves the first-media-entry-only skip rule, one-second key search,
preroll timestamp backfill and original TTS cursor exhaustion. Expanded typed
indexes and working buffers are bounded before allocation. This extends the
older single-edit scope description above; general MOV compatibility is open.

AAC edit search at signed64 boundaries follows the pinned mov_fix_index
search_timestamp-minus-time_scale result before FFMAX with the original first
DTS. Native boundary fixtures distinguish final-packet/no-skip behavior from
unbounded bigint subtraction; reconstructed output timestamps remain checked.

Independent AAC sample/track clocks use pinned mov_finalize_packet and demux.c
sample-unit conversions, floored nominal packet timing and MOV's unconverted
initial skip accumulation. Positive STTS sums limit MDHD duration as in
mov_read_stts. This extends earlier matching-clock-only AAC scope.

AAC-LC sample-rate/channel precedence over sample-entry words follows pinned
libavformat/isom.c ff_mp4_read_dec_config_descr and mov.c audio entry parsing.
Valid supported ASC supplies zero or differing header words; source header
bytes and raw container channel count remain separately accessible.

AAC-LC configuration parsing accepts the explicit24-bit frequency form in
addition to indexed frequencies, with bounded bit reads and unchanged
channel/frame-length semantics. The Native reference for container/copy tests
uses pinned libavformat APIs because ffprobe's additional AAC decoder open
rejects explicit-frequency ASC. This does not claim AAC decoding support.

Zero AAC explicit frequency is retained as unset codec metadata; fallback to
MDHD time_scale when greater than1 follows pinned mov_finalize_stsd_codec.
Unresolved zero-rate streams remain demuxable. Nominal duration filling is
suppressed when no sample rate is known,as in demux.c compute_frame_duration.
